{
 "id": "2022-stobbe-birdsongs",
 "name": "Birdsongs vs Traffic Noise: Mood & Paranoia (soundscape listening)",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Stobbe, Sundermann, Ascone & Kühn (2022), \"Birdsongs alleviate anxiety and paranoia in healthy participants\", Scientific Reports 12: 16414. A randomised online between-subject experiment: participants rate their current mood and state paranoia, are assigned at random to one of four 6-minute soundscapes (traffic noise low diversity, traffic noise high diversity, birdsongs low diversity, birdsongs high diversity), listen with headphones at about 80% volume, rate mood and paranoia again, and finally rate how diverse/monotonous, beautiful and pleasant the soundscape was on 0–100 sliders. The player cannot be skipped and Continue unlocks only when the recording has ended; the page records the seconds actually played. Pre and post answers are keyed \"pre\" / \"post\". Original result (N = 295, Prolific, German-speaking): traffic noise increased depression (small effect for low, medium for high diversity), high-diversity birdsong reduced depression, and both birdsong soundscapes reduced anxiety and paranoia (medium effects); no effects on digit span or dual n-back. Sounds: the authors' four soundscapes from OSF https://osf.io/4y3vh/ (built from xeno-canto and freesound recordings; no licence stated on OSF, used with the source named and removable on request). Differences from the original study: (1) mood is measured with six short state items written for this template (tense, worried, nervous; sad, down, cheerful reversed) on the STADI's 1–4 format instead of the copyrighted STADI state anxiety and depression scales; (2) the three brief state Paranoia Checklist items are quoted from the paper, agreement only, 1–11; (3) the digit span and dual n-back tasks and the CAPE psychosis-liability screening are omitted; (4) listening compliance is checked by recorded play time instead of typing a spoken two-digit code; (5) English instead of German; education and income questions omitted.",
 "author": "Stobbe et al.",
 "year": "2022",
